<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.13.7: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01090</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.7: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c94482b7-f9af-4b1c-9614-89c46efdc448</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Display several familiar graphs of functions from this unit. Here are some examples.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A graph. "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8e212f8010016b9ef2ed559709268813ad1477b2"/></media></figure></para>
<para><figure><media alt="2 graphs. Horizontal axis, 0 to 7, time in seconds. Vertical axis, 0 to 50 by 10’s, height in meters. Graph R is a parabolic and opens down. Graph D is piecewise linear."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/381549c4047fb4e339394723978be30242415483"/></media></figure></para>
<para><figure><media alt="piecewise function"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/99caf2b6fcd0515576213be1c5522056b67d4f4c"/></media></figure></para>
<para><figure><media alt="squiggly line graphing distance from post over time"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/906f1b15379d92e1b23fe228286d83ddaa06ecf4"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Ask students to examine the graphs and use them to help summarize what graphs can tell us about the domain and range of functions. Ask students to complete the following prompts as thoroughly as they can.</para>
<list>
  <item>
    <para>I can learn about the domain and range of a function from a graph by looking for . . .</para>
  </item>
  <item>
    <para>A graph may not always show all that is needed to fully describe the domain and range, however. For example, it may not show . . .</para>
  </item>
</list></section>
</content>
